e] [17592186363924 1113 #uuid "c9f9f6ec-3614-51f5-8a8c-4b576b94a5aa" 13194139535734 true] [17592186363924 1145 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(2-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2-3y,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-3y)=-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{1}(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x)\\cdot ((2-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{e}^{-3x}\\cdot ((2-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" 13194139535734 true] [17592186363925 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363926 13194139535734 true] [17592186363925 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363927 13194139535734 true] [17592186363925 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363928 13194139535734 true] [17592186363925 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363929 13194139535734 true] [17592186363925 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535734 true] [17592186363925 1041 "field-1" 13194139535734 true] [17592186363925 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363927 13194139535734 true] [17592186363925 1110 #uuid "fed192f1-d7ca-5c64-b0e8-23a0458dd608" 13194139535734 true] 